<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siddhi Narasimha Malla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1620–<a href="/years/1661">1661</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siddis">Siddhi</a> Narasimha Malla was a Malla king of Lalitpur (Patan) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, ruling from about 1620 until 1661 as head of a separate kingdom alongside Kantipur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>. Known for his piety, he built the stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> Mandir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-durbar-square">Patan Durbar Square</a>, completed in 1637, and is said to have left the throne to become an ascetic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siddhi-narasimha-malla.jpg" alt="Siddhi Narasimha Malla" width="320" height="651" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alina Chan · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_the_Nepalese_King_Siddhi_Narasimha_Malla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
